        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What an Electric Snow Shovel Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Strip the marketing away and an electric snow shovel, also sold as a power shovel or cordless snow shovel, is a single-stage snow thrower shrunk down to the width of a large hand shovel. A small motor spins a rubber or plastic paddle auger inside a housing that is typically 10 to 13 inches wide. The auger scoops snow off the surface and flings it out of a fixed or adjustable chute, usually forward and to one side. There is no second stage, no impeller and no drive system. You provide all the forward motion by pushing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The shape tells you how it is meant to be used. Most have a long shaft with a second handle partway down, so you hold it like a string trimmer or a leaf blower rather than walking behind it. That is why some retailers file them under handheld snow blowers. Weight is usually in the low to mid teens of pounds for corded models, and somewhat more once a battery is clipped on, which is light enough to carry up a flight of stairs or lift onto a deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearing depth is the number that matters most and the one most often glossed over. Manufacturers commonly list a maximum depth of around 4 to 6 inches per pass. That is not a soft limit you can push through with effort. It is roughly the height of the intake opening, and snow deeper than that simply piles up in front of the housing. You can take deeper snow in layers, skimming the top half off and then going back for the bottom, but that doubles the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Throw distance is the second spec on every listing, usually quoted somewhere between 15 and 25 feet. Treat those figures as best-case numbers for dry, fluffy snow. Wet snow leaves the chute heavier and lands much closer, and a gusty wind can blow light powder straight back at you. What you can rely on is that a power shovel moves snow off the path and well clear of it, which is the whole point. You are not lifting and tossing each scoop by hand.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE DEFINITION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A power shovel is a 12-inch single-stage snow thrower you carry.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Judge it against that, not against a 21- or 24-inch blower. It wins on weight, storage and reach, and loses on width, depth and heavy snow every time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-clears" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What an Electric Snow Shovel Clears Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Steps are where a power shovel justifies its price on its own. A snow blower cannot climb stairs, and clearing them by hand means bending and lifting on the worst possible footing. A power shovel can be carried up the steps and run across each tread in a pass or two, throwing the snow off the side. The same goes for porches and stoops, where the area is too small to bother wheeling out a full machine but too awkward to shovel comfortably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decks are the second strong case. Clearing a deck keeps snow load off the structure and stops melt-and-freeze cycles from soaking the boards, but most people will not drive a heavy two-stage machine onto wood decking, and the steel scraper bar on many blowers can gouge it. A light power shovel with a rubber-tipped auger is a far gentler tool. Owners commonly report that it is the job they reach for theirs most often after the walkway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walkways, side paths and the strip between the driveway and the front door are the classic use. These runs are often 2 to 4 feet wide, which a 12-inch shovel covers in a handful of passes. On a short single-car driveway in a region that gets frequent light snowfalls, a power shovel can genuinely be the only tool you need, as long as you get out early and often. The same applies to a patio, a path out to the shed, or a dog run in the back yard.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best conditions are fresh, dry snow up to a few inches deep on a smooth paved or wood surface. In that range, a power shovel is fast, quiet compared with gas, and noticeably easier on your back than a manual shovel. It also handles the thin skim of snow a full blower leaves behind poorly, because the rubber paddles contact the surface and sweep it fairly clean. That makes it a useful finishing tool even in a household that already owns a bigger machine.
          </p>

